[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer**

*Use a **`color-relief`** layer.*  
It reads the same `raster-dem` source you already have and lets you paint the DEM directly with an **`["elevation"]`** expression. The colour ramp is built with an `interpolate` expression that maps elevation (in metres) to the colours you want (blue → green → brown → white).

Below is a complete, ready-to-copy example that shows:

1. Adding the DEM source (`type: "raster-dem"`).  
2. Adding a `color-relief` layer that tints the terrain with a hypsometric colour ramp.  
3. Optional tweaks (opacity, resampling, exaggeration, sky) so you can see the effect clearly.

---

## 1. Add the DEM source (if you haven’t already)

```js
// Assuming you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map`
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // URL pattern for your DEM tiles (any format MapLibre can decode)
  url: 'https://my.tileserver.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // optional – tell MapLibre how the DEM is encoded (terrarium, mapbox, etc.)
  // encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 12               // whatever your source’s max zoom is
});
```

> **Note** – The source is *only* a `raster-dem`. Do **not** add a `raster` layer for it; that would just show the raw RGB values of the DEM.

---

## 2. Add a `color-relief` layer

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hypsometric-tint',
  type: 'color-relief',          // <-- the layer type you need
  source: 'terrain-dem',         // the raster-dem source defined above
  paint: {
    // 1️⃣ The colour ramp – an interpolate expression over ["elevation"]
    'color-relief-color': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['elevation'],               // elevation in metres (relative to the DEM’s datum)

      // ---- Elevation (m) → Colour (hex or rgba) ----
      // Sea level and below – deep ocean blues
      -200, '#0d1b2a',   // very deep water (optional, you can start at 0)
      0,    '#0d3b66',   // sea level – dark blue

      // Shallow water → light blue → turquoise
      50,   '#1d3557',
      100,  '#27496d',
      200,  '#3a7ca5',
      400,  '#5c9ead',

      // Low land – greens
      600,  '#7fb069',
      800,  '#a1c96a',
      1200, '#c2d96c',

      // Mid-elevation – yellows / light browns
      1500, '#e5e86c',
      1800, '#e5c96c',
      2100, '#e5a96c',

      // High land – browns → reddish-brown
      2500, '#c96c6c',
      3000, '#a65c5c',
      3500, '#834c4c',

      // Alpine zone – light grey → white
      4000, '#bfbfbf',
      4500, '#e0e0e0',
      5000, '#ffffff'   // peaks
    ],

    // 2️⃣ Optional opacity (0-1). 1 = fully opaque.
    'color-relief-opacity': 0.85,

    // 3️⃣ Optional resampling method (GL JS 5.20+).  "linear" is the default.
    // 'resampling': 'linear'
  }
});
```

### How the ramp works

* `interpolate` – creates a smooth gradient between the stops you define.  
* `["linear"]` – linear interpolation (you can also use `["exponential", 1.5]` for a non-linear feel).  
* `["elevation"]` – the only expression that returns the DEM’s elevation **inside** a `color-relief` paint property.  
* The numbers after the colour stops are **elevation values in metres** (relative to the DEM’s vertical datum). Adjust them to match the range of your DEM.

You can freely add, remove, or reorder stops to get the exact palette you want. A typical hypsometric scheme goes:

```
deep blue → light blue → green → yellow → brown → white
```

but you can also insert intermediate shades (e.g., a “sienna” for sub-alpine terrain) or use `rgba()` if you need transparency.

---

## 3. (Optional) Make the 3-D effect visible

If you also want to see the terrain in 3-D, enable it and tilt the camera:

```js
// Turn the DEM into a 3-D mesh
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  exaggeration: 1.0   // increase >1 to exaggerate relief
});

// Pitch the map so you can see the height
map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, bearing: 0, duration: 1500 });

// Add a sky so the horizon isn’t just the page background
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#cceeff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

> **Important:** The `color-relief` layer works **independently** of `setTerrain()`.  
> *If you only need a 2-D hypsometric map, you can skip `setTerrain` and the sky.*  
> *If you enable terrain, the colour-relief tint will drape over the 3-D mesh automatically.*

---

## 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hypsometric colour-relief</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add sources programmatically
      layers: []            // same for layers
    },
    center: [0, 45],
    zoom: 4,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://my.tileserver.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // 2️⃣ Colour-relief layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hypsometric-tint',
      type: 'color-relief',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'color-relief-color': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['elevation'],
          -200, '#0d1b2a',
          0,    '#0d3b66',
          50,   '#1d3557',
          100,  '#27496d',
          200,  '#3a7ca5',
          400,  '#5c9ead',
          600,  '#7fb069',
          800,  '#a1c96a',
          1200, '#c2d96c',
          1500, '#e5e86c',
          1800, '#e5c96c',
          2100, '#e5a96c',
          2500, '#c96c6c',
          3000, '#a65c5c',
          3500, '#834c4c',
          4000, '#bfbfbf',
          4500, '#e0e0e0',
          5000, '#ffffff'
        ],
        'color-relief-opacity': 0.85
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ (optional) 3-D terrain + sky
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
    map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, duration: 1200 });
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': '#cceeff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser that supports WebGL (desktop Chrome/Firefox/Edge, or recent mobile browsers) and you’ll see the DEM coloured directly on the client, with no pre-rendered coloured tiles.

---

## 5. Quick checklist – “Did I do it right?”

| ✅ | Check |
|---|-------|
| **Layer type** | `type: "color-relief"` (not `hillshade`, `raster`, `fill`, …) |
| **Source** | Same `raster-dem` source you already use (`type: "raster-dem"`) |
| **Ramp expression** | `["interpolate", ["linear"], ["elevation"], …]` inside `color-relief-color` |
| **Opacity** | Set `color-relief-opacity` if you want the underlying imagery to show through |
| **Resampling** (optional) | `resampling: "linear"` (default) or `"nearest"` if you need a pixelated look |
| **No pre-rendered tiles** | All colour work is done client-side; you can change the ramp on-the-fly |
| **3-D (optional)** | `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})` + `pitch > 0` + `map.setSky()` for a nice horizon |

If you follow the snippet above, you’ll have a fully client-side hypsometric map that works on any device that can run MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!

## Short answer  
Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**.  
MapLibre GL JS itself does not generate contours, but the companion library **`maplibre-contour`** does the heavy lifting in a Web-Worker and feeds the results back to the map as a virtual vector source.  

Below is a complete, step-by-step recipe (with a minimal working example) that you can drop into any MapLibre GL JS project.

---

## 1. What you need

| Item | Why it’s required |
|------|-------------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS** (≥ v2.4, the version that ships the `color-relief` and `hillshade` layers) | Core map engine |
| **`maplibre-contour`** npm package (or a bundled copy) | Generates contour tiles from a DEM on the client |
| A **`raster-dem` source** that serves elevation tiles (e.g. Terrarium, Mapbox, or MapTiler DEM) | Input data for the contour generator |
| (Optional) **Web-Worker** support – most browsers have it, but you can disable it for debugging | Runs the contour algorithm off the UI thread |

> **Note** – The `maplibre-contour` library is **not** part of the core MapLibre bundle; you must import it yourself. It works with any DEM that MapLibre can read, as long as you tell it the correct `encoding` (`terrarium`, `mapbox`, `raw`, …).

---

## 2. Install the library

```bash
# Using npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour
# or
yarn add maplibre-contour
```

If you are not using a bundler, you can grab the pre-built UMD file from the repo’s `dist/` folder and load it with a `<script>` tag:

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.umd.js"></script>
```

The global variable will be `maplibreContour`.

---

## 3. Create the DEM source (the same one you would use for hillshade/terrain)

```javascript
// 1️⃣ Add the raster-dem source to the map
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', // any tilejson or URL template
  tileSize: 256,
  // IMPORTANT: match the encoding of the source tiles
  //   'terrarium'  – 16-bit packed as R*256 + G + B/256 (MapTiler default)
  //   'mapbox'    – 16-bit signed integer (Mapbox DEM)
  //   'raw'       – 32-bit float in the red channel (some custom pipelines)
  //   'heightmap' – 8-bit grayscale (rare)
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 12
});
```

> **Why `encoding` matters** – The contour generator must decode the same way MapLibre does, otherwise the elevation values will be off.

---

## 4. Spin up a `DemSource` from `maplibre-contour`

```javascript
import { DemSource } from 'maplibre-contour';

// 2️⃣ Create the contour-generation helper
const demSource = new DemSource({
  // URL pattern must match the raster-dem source above
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Must be identical to the source’s encoding
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // The highest zoom you expect to request contours for
  maxzoom: 12,
  // Set to true to run the heavy work in a Web-Worker (recommended)
  worker: true
});

// 3️⃣ Register the helper with the MapLibre instance
demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);
```

`DemSource` does **not** add a layer itself; it only registers a custom URL protocol (`contour://`) that the map can treat as a normal vector source.

---

## 5. Add a *virtual* vector source that points to the contour protocol

```javascript
// 4️⃣ Add a vector source that will be filled by the contour worker
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is generated for each zoom level you care about.
  // You can pass a single URL (the library will replace {z}/{x}/{y} for you)
  // or an array of URLs – we use the helper to build the URL.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour spacing (in metres) per zoom level ──
      //   key = zoom, value = [minorInterval, majorInterval]
      thresholds: {
        // At zoom 10 we want 200 m minor, 1000 m major lines
        10: [200, 1000],
        // At zoom 12 we want finer detail
        12: [100, 500],
        // At zoom 14 we go even finer
        14: [50, 200]
      },
      // Property names that will appear on each feature
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // numeric elevation (m)
      levelKey: 'level',     // 1 = major, 0 = minor (useful for styling)
      // Optional: give the source a stable name for caching
      contourLayer: 'contours'
    })
  ],
  // The contour tiles are generated up to this zoom (must be ≤ DEM maxzoom)
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

**What the protocol does** – When the map asks for a tile like `contour://0/0/0`, the library:

1. Loads the underlying DEM tiles for that area.
2. Runs a marching-squares / isoline algorithm in a worker.
3. Emits a **vector tile** (MVT) containing line features with the properties you defined (`ele`, `level`).

Because the result is a true vector source, you can style it exactly like any other line layer.

---

## 6. Add a line layer (and optionally a label layer)

```javascript
// 5️⃣ Draw the contour lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours', // the name we gave in contourProtocolUrl()
  paint: {
    // Light, semi-transparent black works well over imagery
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)',
    // Thicker line for major contours (level === 1)
    'line-width': [
      'case',
      ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      1.2, // major
      0.6  // minor
    ]
  }
});

// Optional: label the major contours
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // only major lines
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-size': 10,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

That’s it – the map will now draw contour lines **on-the-fly** as you pan/zoom.

---

## 7. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre runtime contours</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.umd.js"></script>
<script>
  // 0️⃣ Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add everything programmatically
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: -17
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the DEM source (used later by terrain, hillshade, etc.)
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // 2️⃣ Set up the contour helper
    const { DemSource } = maplibreContour; // global from UMD build
    const demSource = new DemSource({
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      encoding: 'terrarium',
      maxzoom: 12,
      worker: true
    });
    demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl);

    // 3️⃣ Add the virtual vector source
    map.addSource('contours', {
      type: 'vector',
      tiles: [
        demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
          thresholds: {10:[200,1000],12:[100,500],14:[50,200]},
          elevationKey: 'ele',
          levelKey: 'level',
          contourLayer: 'contours'
        })
      ],
      maxzoom: 15
    });

    // 4️⃣ Draw the lines
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      paint: {
        'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)',
        'line-width': [
          'case',
          ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
          1.2,
          0.6
        ]
      }
    });

    // 5️⃣ (Optional) Labels for major contours
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'contour-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'contours',
      'source-layer': 'contours',
      filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
      layout: {
        'symbol-placement': 'line',
        'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
        'text-size': 10,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#222',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, pan/zoom, and you’ll see contour lines appear instantly – no pre-generated vector tiles required.

