## 8. Things to keep in mind

| Issue | Why it happens | How to avoid / fix |
|-------|----------------|--------------------|
| **Wrong elevation values** | `encoding` mismatch (e.g. DEM is `mapbox` but you told the library `terrarium`). | Verify the DEM’s encoding (check the tile provider docs) and set the same string in both the `raster-dem` source **and** the `DemSource` options. |
| **No contours at high zoom** | `maxzoom` of the `DemSource` or the virtual vector source is too low. | Set `maxzoom` on the `DemSource` to the highest zoom you need (≤ the DEM source’s maxzoom) and give the vector source a `maxzoom` ≥ that value. |
| **Stuttery UI** | Running the algorithm on the main thread (`worker: false`). | Keep `worker: true` (default) – the library ships a tiny Web-Worker that does the marching-squares work off-screen. |
| **Too many lines / performance drop** | Very fine `thresholds` (e.g. 5 m spacing) at high zoom. | Choose a sensible spacing per zoom; the `thresholds` map lets you coarsen the lines as you zoom out. |
| **Missing line features** | The `source-layer` name is wrong. | The protocol always emits a single layer named whatever you passed as `contourLayer` (default `"contours"`). Use that exact string in `source-layer`. |
| **Contours disappear after style reload** | The custom protocol isn’t re-registered. | Call `demSource.setupMaplibre(maplibregl)` **once** after the map is created (or after any `map.setStyle()` that wipes sources). |
| **You need hillshade *and* contours** | Both can share the same DEM source. | Add a `hillshade` layer that points to `'terrain-dem

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the code works with **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.5** (the version that introduced the `sky` object).

---

## 1. What you need to have already

```js
// 1️⃣  A MapLibre map instance
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,               // you already have this
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true          // recommended for 3-D
});

// 2️⃣  A raster-dem source (the same one you use for terrain)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // ← your DEM tileset
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 12,
  encoding: 'terrarium'   // or whatever your DEM uses
});
```

If you already called `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})` you can skip the source-add step – just make sure the source name matches.

---

## 2. Turn the terrain on (if you haven’t already)

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 3️⃣  Enable the 3-D mesh
  map.setTerrain({
    source: 'terrain-dem',
    exaggeration: 1   // increase only after you’re happy with the look
  });

  // … now add the sky (next step)
});
```

> **Why the sky must be added *after* `setTerrain`?**  
> The sky is rendered **behind** the terrain mesh. If you call `setSky` before the terrain exists, the sky will be drawn but you’ll never see it because the map’s background layer (or a solid `background` paint) will sit on top of it.

---

## 3. Add a sky object (the “real sky”)

```js
// Still inside the same `map.on('load', …)` callback
map.setSky({
  // ---- colour of the dome at the zenith (top of the sky)
  'sky-color': '#87CEEB',          // light sky-blue

  // ---- colour of the horizon line where sky meets ground
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',      // crisp white

  // How quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon
  // 0 = abrupt, 1 = very gradual
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

  // ---- fog (haze) that appears where the terrain meets the sky
  // The fog colour is what you’ll see “through” the distant terrain.
  // It should be a slightly desaturated version of the sky colour.
  'fog-color': '#cce0ff',          // pale blue-gray

  // How much the fog colour blends into the horizon line
  // 0 = no fog at the horizon, 1 = horizon fully fogged
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,

  // How quickly the fog fades as it approaches the ground
  // 0 = fog only at the very top, 1 = fog reaches the ground
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
});
```

### What each property does

| Property | Effect |
|----------|--------|
| **`sky-color`** | Colour of the sky at the very top (zenith). |
| **`horizon-color`** | Colour exactly at the horizon line. |
| **`sky-horizon-blend`** | Controls the smoothness of the gradient between the two. |
| **`fog-color`** | The colour of the haze that “fills” the distance. |
| **`horizon-fog-blend`** | How much fog is visible right at the horizon. |
| **`fog-ground-blend`** | How far the fog reaches down toward the terrain. |

> **Tip:** Play with the three blend values (`sky-horizon-blend`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) until the transition looks natural for your data and map style. Small changes (0.05-0.1) can make a big visual difference.

---

## 4. Make sure nothing blocks the sky

* **Background layer** – If your style contains a `background` layer with an opaque `background-color`, the sky will be hidden. Either remove that layer or set its colour to `rgba(0,0,0,0)` (transparent).

```js
// Example: make the background transparent
const bg = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'background');
if (bg) {
  map.setPaintProperty(bg.id, 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
}
```

* **Layer order** – The sky is a *root-level* object, not a layer, so it automatically sits behind all layers. You only need to worry about the background layer mentioned above.

---

