    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source (replace with your own URL if needed)
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 12,
      encoding: 'terrarium'
    });

    // 2️⃣ Enable terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // 3️⃣ Make background transparent (optional, but avoids a solid colour)
    const bg = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'background');
    if (bg) map.setPaintProperty(bg.id, 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');

    // 4️⃣ Add the sky + fog
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#87CEEB',
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': '#cce0ff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, tilt the map, and you should see:

* A **blue-sky dome** that fades to white at the horizon.  
* A **soft blue-gray haze** that thickens as the terrain recedes, giving the classic “ground-meets-sky” look.  
* The terrain mesh still looks exactly as before, but now the area above the horizon is no longer the page background.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Sky stays black / invisible** | `setTerrain` not called, or terrain source missing. | Verify `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})` runs after the source is added. |
| **Solid colour (white/black) still covers the sky** | A `background` layer with an opaque colour. | Set its `background-color` to transparent or delete the layer. |
| **Fog never appears** | `fog-color` omitted or set to fully transparent. | Provide a non-transparent colour (e.g., `#cce0ff`). |
| **Fog looks too thick or too thin** | Blend values (`horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) are extreme. | Adjust those numbers in small increments (0.05-0.1). |
| **Sky colour looks washed out** | `sky-horizon-blend` too high (makes the gradient too gradual). | Lower it (e.g., 0.3) or pick a more saturated `sky-color`. |
| **Performance drops dramatically** | Terrain already heavy; adding sky is cheap, but a very high `exaggeration` + many draped raster layers can choke low-end devices. | Keep `exaggeration` modest, limit raster overlays, and enable `antialias:true` only if needed. |

---

## 7. Quick reference – the `sky` object spec (MapLibre GL JS)

```json
{
  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "<color>",          // zenith colour
    "horizon-color": "<color>",      // horizon line colour
    "sky-horizon-blend": <0-1>,       // gradient smoothness
    "fog-color": "<color>",          // haze colour (requires terrain)
    "horizon-fog-blend": <0-1>,      // fog at horizon
    "fog-ground-blend": <0-1>,        // fog depth toward ground
    "atmosphere-blend": <0-1>         // (optional) extra atmospheric effect, default 0
  }
}
```

All of these keys are **optional** – any you omit will fall back to the defaults shown in the spec.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Enable terrain** (`map.setTerrain({source:'your-dem'})`).  
2. **Make the background transparent** (or remove the background layer).  
3. **Call `map.setSky()`** with a `sky-color`, `horizon-color`, and fog settings (`fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`).  
4. Adjust the blend values until the sky-to-ground transition looks natural.

That’s it – you now have a proper sky and haze that move with the camera, giving your 3-D map a polished, realistic look. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for putting a pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap (plain JPEG/PNG tiles) behind all of your vector data and label layers in MapLibre GL JS.

---

## 1.  What you are adding

* **Source type:** `raster` (not `raster-dem`).  
* **Layer type:** `raster`.  
* **What it does:** draws the JPEG tiles exactly as they are – no hill-shade calculations, no terrain mesh, no elevation expressions.  
* **Why it works for you:** you already have the relief baked into the imagery, so you only need to make sure the layer sits at the bottom of the style stack.

---

## 2.  Minimal code – “quick-start”

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Relief basemap + vector data</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style that already contains your vector
  //     data / label layers – e.g. a MapLibre “streets” style)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // <-- replace with your own style
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the raster-relief source
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: [
        // 👉  Replace with the URL template you actually serve
        //     {z}/{x}/{y}.jpg  (or .png, .webp, etc.)
        'https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,               // most raster tiles are 256 px
      // optional but useful:
      // minzoom: 0,
      // maxzoom: 14,
      // attribution: '© My Relief Tileset'
    });

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add the raster layer – put it *under* everything else
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Find the first layer that you want the basemap to be *below*.
    // In a typical style the first label layer is called something like
    // "road-label" or "place-label".  If you don’t know the id, you can
    // inspect `map.getStyle().layers` in the console.
    const firstLabelLayerId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l =>
      l.type === 'symbol' && l.layout && l.layout['text-field']
    )?.id;

    map.addLayer(
      {
        id: 'relief-basemap',
        type: 'raster',
        source: 'relief-basemap',
        paint: {
          // optional – make the basemap a little transparent so you can see
          // underlying background or a subtle colour tint
          // 'raster-opacity': 0.95,
          // optional – smooth the transition when you zoom/pan
          // 'raster-fade-duration': 300
        }
      },
      // Insert *before* the first label layer (or null to append at the top)
      firstLabelLayerId || undefined
    );

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  (Optional) If you have other vector layers that you added
    //     programmatically after the style load, make sure they are added
    //     *after* the raster layer, e.g.:
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // map.addLayer({ id: 'my-lines', type: 'line', source: 'my-vector', ... });
    // map.moveLayer('my-lines', 'relief-basemap'); // ensures it’s on top

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  Done!  Your relief basemap is now the bottommost layer.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Add source** (`type: 'raster'`) | Tells MapLibre that the tiles are ordinary images. No DEM decoding, no hillshade, no terrain. |
| **Add layer** (`type: 'raster'`) | Renders those images. The `paint` object can control opacity, fade-duration, etc. |
| **Insert before a label layer** | Guarantees the raster sits *under* all symbol (label) layers. If you omit the `before` argument the raster would be added on top of everything, covering your labels. |
| **Move later layers** (optional) | If you add vector layers later, you can call `moveLayer` to keep the ordering correct. |

---

## 3.  Fine-tuning the raster layer

| Property | What it controls | Typical values |
|----------|------------------|----------------|
| `raster-opacity` | Transparency of the basemap (0 = invisible, 1 = opaque) | `0.9` – `1` |
| `raster-fade-duration` | Milliseconds for the cross-fade when tiles change (zoom/pan) | `300` – `500` |
| `raster-resampling` (GL JS ≥ 5.6) | How the image is resampled when scaled (`linear`, `nearest`) | `linear` for smoother zoom |
| `tileSize` | Size of a tile in pixels (most services use 256) | `256` (or `512` for high-res tiles) |
| `maxzoom` / `minzoom` | Limits the zoom levels at which the source is queried | match your tile server’s capabilities |

---

## 4.  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Labels disappear** | Raster layer was added *after* the label layers, covering them. | Insert the raster **before** the first label layer (see code) or call `map.moveLayer('relief-basemap', 'some-label-id')`. |
| **Tile URLs 404** | Wrong URL template (missing `{z}/{x}/{y}` placeholders) or wrong scheme. | Verify the exact pattern your tile server expects; test a single tile in the browser first. |
| **Blurry tiles at high zoom** | Tile size mismatch (`tileSize: 256` but server serves 512 px tiles) or missing `raster-resampling`. | Set `tileSize` to the size your server actually returns, or enable `raster-resampling: 'linear'`. |
| **Background shows through “holes”** | Some tiles are missing (e.g., over ocean) and the map’s background colour shows. | Add a `background` layer *below* the raster (or set `raster-opacity` < 1) to control the colour that appears where tiles are absent. |
| **Performance drop on mobile** | Very large raster tiles (e.g., 1024 px) or many high-resolution layers. | Keep `tileSize` at 256 px, limit `maxzoom` to what you really need, and avoid stacking multiple raster layers. |

---

## 5.  When you *do* want a **dynamic** hillshade or terrain

If later you decide you’d rather generate the relief on the client (so you can also add 3-D terrain, contour lines, etc.), you’ll need a **DEM** (`raster-dem`) source instead of a JPEG basemap. That would involve:

1. Adding a `raster-dem` source that points at a DEM tile service (Terrarium, Mapbox, etc.).
2. Adding a `hillshade` layer (or a `color-relief` layer) that reads the DEM.
3. Optionally calling `map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1})` for 3-D.

But for a *static* pre-rendered relief image, the raster-layer approach above is the correct, simplest solution.

---

## 6.  TL;DR – Minimal snippet you can copy-paste

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣  Raster source (your JPEG tiles)
  map.addSource('relief', {
    type: 'raster',
    tiles: ['https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
    tileSize: 256
  });

  // 2️⃣  Insert raster layer under the first label layer
  const firstLabel = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol' && l.layout?.['text-field'])?.id;
  map.addLayer(
    {
      id: 'relief',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'relief',
      paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1 }
    },
    firstLabel   // ← ensures labels stay on top
  );
});
```

That’s it – your beautiful shaded-relief basemap will now sit quietly beneath all of your vector data and labels. Happy mapping! 🚀
